# Root ignore for the Kiosk reference monorepo (K-552 / T-011 publication hygiene).
#
# This repo is going PUBLIC. Until now it relied on the maintainer's
# machine-global git ignore, so a fresh clone doing `git add -A` would commit
# machine-local and secret-bearing files that no tracked ignore covered. This
# file is the safety net. It is deliberately CONSERVATIVE — it must never hide a
# file that is currently tracked-and-intended (the demos DO track their own
# Gemfile.lock and deploy/env/*.env.example; the demo `mise.toml.example` and the
# gems' *.gemspec are tracked). The per-gem / per-demo .gitignore files still own
# their subtree specifics (db, log, tmp, storage, master.key/credentials).

# ── Toolchain version pins (machine-local; managed per developer) ────────────
# The maintainer uses mise; `mise.toml.example` is the tracked template — the
# real `mise.toml` / `.mise.toml` is local and must not ship.
#
# THIS WAS RE-DECIDED RATHER THAN INHERITED (K-1321). A fresh clone carries no
# tracked Ruby pin of any kind, so an rbenv or asdf user gets no hint from the
# tree — which is a real cost, and it was weighed against tracking one. The
# answer is to keep these ignored and state the supported RANGE instead:
# README.md#install states the supported range, the gemspecs declare the
# floor, and `ci.yml`'s `ruby-floor` job RUNS every gem suite on it, derived
# from a gemspec at job time. A range both ends of which are on the merge gate
# is worth more to a contributor than one pinned patch release that a version
# manager they do not use will silently ignore. The eight `.ruby-version`
# files on the maintainer's disk are `rails new` scaffolding, not a decision.
.mise.toml
mise.toml
.ruby-version
.ruby-gemset

# ── Bundler / local gem installs ─────────────────────────────────────────────
.bundle/
/vendor/bundle/
# Local gem cache dir (matches the maintainer's global ignore). Trailing slash
# so it never touches the tracked *.gemspec files.
.gems/
# `gem build` output. Every gem here builds to <name>-<version>.gem in its own
# directory, and bin/check-gem-packaging builds all 14 on every run — one
# interrupted run leaves the artifacts behind, and a `git add -A` in a repo
# going public would commit a binary package of the source next to it. The glob
# ends in `.gem`, so it does NOT match the tracked `*.gemspec` files (verified:
# `git check-ignore` clears all 14) and nothing currently tracked matches it.
*.gem

# ── Secrets — never publish (defense-in-depth over the per-demo ignores) ─────
# Rails master keys and encrypted credentials, wherever a demo app keeps them.
**/config/master.key
**/config/credentials.yml.enc
**/config/credentials/*.key
# Local environment files (real secrets); *.env.example templates stay tracked.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ── Logs and temp ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
/tmp/

# ── Test / coverage artifacts ────────────────────────────────────────────────
/coverage/
.rspec_status
.rspec-local

# ── Local agent / worktree scratch (maintainer machine) ──────────────────────
**/.claude/settings.local.json
.worktrees/
lefthook-local.yml

# ── Editor / OS cruft ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~
